This page describes a project pertaining to [[physical learning systems]], [[artificial intelligence]], [[biocomputing]] and [[intelligence]].

[[!toc levels=5]]

# Summary

This project proposes a thermodynamic physical learning system in which computation and adaptation emerge directly from the physics of self-organizing analog matter. Using techniques such as nanowire/nanoparticle networks, memristive/ionic media, and viscoelastic conductors, we treat the substrate as a high-dimensional dynamical system that learns via dynamics using fast energy-minimizing relaxation and reward-gated plasticity.

Modern AI scales with the number of adaptive degrees of freedom. The human brain sets today's benchmark with ~8x10^10 neurons and ~10^14 synapses, operating on millisecond timescales with synaptic plasticity spanning milliseconds to minutes. A particle-based analog substrate can, in principle, dwarf this capacity: a dense liquid material of ~100 nm nano-objects (although smaller and larger objects could be used instead) easily reaches 10^10 conductive particles per cm^2; because each particle potentially forms multiple junctions, different (initially random) electrical circuits can form via inter-particle contacts. These systems can trivially scale to million- to billion-fold more adaptive micro-interactions than a neurobiological system of similar footprint. These physical junctions respond at native material timescales: electronic relaxation in picoseconds to nanoseconds, capacitive/RC dynamics in nanoseconds to microseconds, and controlled ionic/structural plasticity in microseconds to milliseconds, thus enabling inference and learning bursts on microsecond or faster horizons. This combination of astronomical state dimensionality plus ultrafast local dynamics provides a new scaling frontier where compute can grow outside the semiconductor supply chain. These substrates compute by relaxing system energy, and if harnessed with hardware-in-the-loop training (black-box RL/evolution, reward-gated consolidation), such substrates could deliver orders-of-magnitude higher model capacity per cm^2, millisecond-to-microsecond training episodes, and exceptional energy efficiency, opening a path to compact, self-organizing learners that outscale digital architectures in both density and latency. Well, it may be able to do that, but only after the following feasibility assessments are completed...

# another summary

The objective of this project is to develop a **three-dimensional physical material that can be trained to perform machine-learning computations through changes occurring within the material itself**. Rather than representing a neural network explicitly in silicon or software, the material would exploit a large disordered network of nonlinear, history-dependent electrical junctions whose conductances, relaxation times, and internal states evolve in response to electrical stimulation and eventually to training or reward signals. Silver-based atomic-switch and diffusive-memristor systems are particularly relevant because Ag/Ag₂S and related Ag-in-insulator systems exhibit volatile switching, persistent switching, short-term memory, and transitions toward longer-lived states; nanoparticle and nanowire networks have separately demonstrated reservoir-computing, temporal-memory, classification, and sequence-processing tasks. The stronger goal of this project is to move beyond using such a material merely as a fixed reservoir with a digitally trained readout and determine whether the physical network itself can acquire useful internal organization through local, physically realizable training mechanisms.

One proposed baseline material is a **3D PVA/water hydrogel containing Ag/Ag₂S nanoparticles, commodity polymer or silica microspheres, and a controlled aqueous electrolyte**, with glycerol optionally added to modify solvent dynamics. PVA is attractive as the structural chassis because it is inexpensive, widely available, water-compatible, electrically insulating, capable of incorporating particles and nanoparticles, and highly tunable through polymer concentration, molecular weight, hydrolysis state, and freeze/thaw processing. The Ag/Ag₂S particles provide the primary nonlinear and adaptive electrical junctions, while micron-scale polystyrene, silica, glass, agarose, or functionalized molecular-biology beads provide an independent means of controlling three-dimensional packing, excluded volume, local particle concentration, pore structure, and chemical microenvironments. A subset of beads could carry proteins, peptides, enzymes, or nucleic acids, allowing the mechanical scaffold, active electrical network, mesoscale geometry, and molecular surface chemistry to be varied semi-independently. Agarose would be maintained as a parallel, biologically gentle host for experiments in which preservation of fragile biomolecular activity is more important than the larger mechanical design space offered by PVA.

The resulting system has a deliberately large experimental design space. Physical variables include Ag/Ag₂S concentration relative to percolation, particle size and shell properties, PVA concentration and crosslinking history, microsphere diameter and loading, mixtures of bead sizes, glycerol content, temperature, water activity, and electrical conductivity. Chemical variables include pH, ionic strength, electrolyte identity, and buffer identity; MOPS is a useful near-neutral starting buffer because it interacts comparatively weakly with Ag(I), whereas Tris, chloride, phosphate, Ca²⁺ and related species should be regarded as active experimental perturbations because they can alter Ag⁺ complexation, precipitation, aggregation, or particle surface charge. Silver surface chemistry can be varied using citrate, PVP, proteins, peptides, or oligonucleotides; citrate- and PVP-coated Ag particles, for example, respond quite differently to pH and ionic-strength changes. Broader screens could test BSA, gelatin, casein, lysozyme, simple peptides, and functionalized beads before moving to directed selection. Silver-binding peptides can be isolated by phage display, providing a route to evolve surface modifiers for a desired *material phenotype* rather than simply maximum Ag affinity. Aptamers and designed DNA provide another large sequence space because Ag⁺ can participate in highly specific metal-mediated nucleic-acid interactions such as C–Ag–C base pairs, potentially allowing nucleic acids to control local Ag⁺ availability, particle association, or junction kinetics.

simulation: A corresponding simulator should initially reproduce the computationally important degrees of freedom rather than every molecular detail. A tractable model would generate a three-dimensional random distribution of Ag/Ag₂S particles and microspheres, construct the electrically accessible particle network, and represent each nearby Ag junction with a small stochastic state model containing variables such as conductance, filament state, switching barrier, local ionic availability, and one or more relaxation processes. Kirchhoff equations would determine instantaneous voltages and currents throughout the network, while junction states evolve forward in time according to voltage-, temperature-, and history-dependent kinetics; microspheres would modify geometry and excluded volume, while buffer, coatings, proteins, peptides, or aptamers would initially enter as experimentally calibrated changes to switching rates, ion availability, aggregation propensity, barrier distributions, or relaxation times. This modeling strategy is consistent with prior work showing that Ag diffusion and regrouping can generate useful synaptic dynamics and with experiments demonstrating that disordered nanoparticle and nanowire networks can support temporal computation. The first purpose of the simulator would therefore not be to predict the exact chemistry of a formulation, but to ask whether plausible ranges of these physical parameters produce a material susceptible to useful ML training: delayed-memory tasks, nonlinear temporal transformations, sequence classification, delayed XOR, or similar benchmarks could be used to determine which combinations of connectivity, nonlinear switching, transient memory, persistent state, and local adaptation justify fabrication and measurement in the laboratory.

# Contracting projects

## Contract project: Analog electronics and physics

1) Seeking a hands-on physicist/EE/materials scientist to assess the feasibility of different thermodynamic physical learning system concepts. You'll evaluate device physics and instrumentation: junction/nonlinear conduction mechanisms, limits, bandwidth, noise/SNR, thermal noise effects, stability/drift, thermal budget, heating/cooling, and device lifetime under various stimulation or energy assumptions. Deliverables include: general feasibility analysis, brainstorming, hypothesizing, basic research/literature review, high-level sketches of simulations if feasible. Ideal backgrounds: physics, analog electronics, electrochemistry, materials science, emulsions or colloids, high-frequency signal generation/processing/ADC, high voltage electronics. This is a short-term contract or project that could turn potentially into an experimental phase. We are separately also pursuing a short-term contract for the ML side of this project.

## Contract project: Machine learning expertise

2) Looking for an [[ML|machine learning]] researcher/engineer to assess the feasibility and directions for training a black box physical learning system consisting of a high-dimensional analog substrate driven and read only at its electrodes. Deliverables include: general feasability analysis, brainstorming, hypothesizing, basic research/literature review, high-level sketches of GPU-based simulations if feasible. Ideal backgrounds: machine learning, reinforcement learning, evolutionary optimization, physical reservoir computing, energy-based/predictive-coding methods, hybrid analog-digital pipelines, and practical experience optimizing noisy non-differentiable systems.

# relevant manuscripts

1. **Srikimkaew, O. et al. (2024). “Volatile and Nonvolatile Dual-Mode Switching Operations in an Ag–Ag₂S Core-Shell Nanoparticle Atomic Switch Network.” Advanced Electronic Materials 10, 2300709.** Demonstrates controllable volatile/nonvolatile operation in Ag/Ag₂S nanoparticle networks and attributes switching to Ag filament formation within Ag₂S.

2. **Srikimkaew, O. et al. (2024). “Short-Term and Long-Term Memory Functionality of a Brain-Like Device Built from Nanoparticle Atomic Switch Networks.” Advanced Electronic Materials.** Shows spontaneous relaxation, paired-pulse effects, and repeated-stimulation transition toward longer-lived potentiation in Ag/Ag₂S nanoparticle networks.

3. **Wang, Z. et al. (2017). “Memristors with Diffusive Dynamics as Synaptic Emulators for Neuromorphic Computing.” Nature Materials 16, 101–108. DOI 10.1038/nmat4756.** Combines experiments, in-situ microscopy, and particle-dynamics simulation to connect Ag redistribution and spontaneous relaxation with short- and long-term synaptic dynamics.

4. **Mallinson, J. B. et al. (2024). “[Experimental Demonstration of Reservoir Computing with Self-Assembled Percolating Networks of Nanoparticles](https://advanced.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/adma.202402319).” Advanced Materials 36, 2402319.** Demonstrates memory, nonlinear computation, time-series processing, and spoken-digit recognition using physical percolating nanoparticle networks.

5. **Zhu, R. et al. (2023). “Online Dynamical Learning and Sequence Memory with Neuromorphic Nanowire Networks.” Nature Communications 14, 6697.** Demonstrates online learning from physical-network dynamics and sequence-memory behavior, providing an important computational benchmark even though training remains associated with the readout rather than purely local material learning.

6. **Lilak, S. et al. (2021). “Spoken Digit Classification by In-Materio Reservoir Computing With Neuromorphic Atomic Switch Networks.” Frontiers in Nanotechnology 3, 675792.** Demonstrates temporal audio classification using the intrinsic dynamics of silver-based atomic-switch networks.

7. **Hassan, C. M. et al./review literature summarized in “Freeze/Thawed Polyvinyl Alcohol Hydrogels: Present, Past and Future” (2022), European Polymer Journal 164, 110974.** Reviews freeze/thaw PVA hydrogels, including control of mechanical and physicochemical properties through polymer characteristics and processing and their incorporation of nanoparticles and additional components.

8. **El Badawy, A. M. et al. (2010). “Impact of Environmental Conditions (pH, Ionic Strength, and Electrolyte Type) on the Surface Charge and Aggregation of Silver Nanoparticle Suspensions.” Environmental Science & Technology 44, 1260–1266.** Establishes strong effects of pH, ionic strength, Ca²⁺, coating chemistry, and electrolyte environment on AgNP aggregation, including the contrast between citrate/electrostatically stabilized and PVP/sterically stabilized particles.

9. **Babel, L. et al. (2020). “Appropriate Buffers for Studying the Bioinorganic Chemistry of Silver(I).” Chemistry 2, 193–202.** Quantifies Ag(I) interactions with common biological buffers and identifies MOPS as comparatively weakly coordinating near neutral pH, while Tris interacts much more strongly.

10. **Li et al. / silver-binding peptide literature; e.g. “Biomimetic Synthesis Silver Crystallite by Peptide AYSSGAPPMPPF Immobilized on PET Film in Vitro” (2005), Journal of Inorganic Biochemistry 99, 1692–1697.** Demonstrates a peptide identified through phage-display-based silver affinity that can control interactions with silver surfaces, supporting peptide evolution as a surface-engineering strategy.

11. **Ono, A. et al. (2008). “Specific Interactions Between Silver(I) Ions and Cytosine–Cytosine Pairs in DNA Duplexes.” Chemical Communications, 4825–4827; and subsequent structural work.** Establishes specific C–Ag–C interactions, providing a mechanistic basis for using designed DNA or evolved aptamers as local Ag⁺-binding and surface-chemistry modifiers.

12. **Nam, J. et al. (2026). “Overcoming Volatility in Ion Gel via Ag Doping for Nonvolatile Memristive Switching.” ACS Omega 11, 5306–5314.** Demonstrates that embedding Ag nanoparticles in an ion-containing polymer matrix can strongly modify retention and synaptic-like dynamics, supporting the broader idea that the surrounding ionic/polymeric medium is an active design variable rather than a passive scaffold.

for MNIST OCR digit recognition task:

| Paper | Task | Physical substrate | Reported result | Where learning occurs |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Zhu et al., 2023 — “Online dynamical learning and sequence memory with neuromorphic nanowire networks”** | **MNIST handwritten digits** | Physical **Ag₂Se nanowire network**, 16-electrode device | **93.4%** with five physical readout channels | Material supplies nonlinear/history-dependent features; external digital **recursive least-squares** classifier is trained online. This is probably the cleanest experimental “OCR-like” network example for our purposes. |
| **Milano et al., 2022 — “In materia reservoir computing with a fully memristive architecture based on self-organizing nanowire networks”** | **MNIST handwritten digits** plus simpler spatiotemporal patterns | Self-organized **Ag nanowires with insulating polymer shells**, coupled to a memristive readout | ≈**90.4% MNIST** in the scaled implementation | Important qualification: the complete MNIST result used an **extended simulated NW network**, whereas physical network experiments established the underlying dynamics and smaller pattern tasks; the readout was implemented/mapped to memristive hardware. |
| **Midya et al., 2019 — “Reservoir Computing Using Diffusive Memristors”** | **MNIST handwritten digits** | Physical **Ag-doped SiOₓ diffusive memristor** reservoir + drift-memristor 1T1R readout | **83%** on 10,000 MNIST test images | Especially interesting because the reservoir is Ag-based and the **readout weights themselves are trained physically in memristors**. The reservoir itself remains untrained. |
| **Du et al., 2017 — “Reservoir computing using dynamic memristors for temporal information processing”** | **MNIST handwritten digits** | Array of physical dynamic memristors | **88.1%** experimentally with 88 reservoir devices | Physical reservoir; trained logistic-regression readout. Input images are converted into temporal pulse sequences. |
| **Ferrarese Lupi et al., 2025 — “Neuromorphic Light-Responsive Organic Matter for in Materia Reservoir Computing”** | **MNIST handwritten digits** | Commercial **PAZO azopolymer**, stimulated optically | ≈**91.4%** | Experimental responses to the 16 possible four-bit temporal sequences were measured and then used to construct MNIST reservoir representations; a software 196×10 ANN readout was trained. So it is physical-material RC, but not 70,000 complete images individually passed through the material. |
| **Lilak et al., 2021 — “Spoken Digit Classification by In-Materio Reservoir Computing With Neuromorphic Atomic Switch Networks”** | **Spoken digits, FSDD** | Physical **Ag nanowire atomic-switch network with AgI junctions** | High spoken-digit accuracy; later thesis description reports about **95%** | MFCC audio features are physically transformed by the AgI network; an external linear-regression readout is fitted. The original paper also notes that only 12 unique input streams were used in that experiment, so it is a smaller benchmark than the later Mallinson study. |
| **Usami et al., 2021 — “In-Materio Reservoir Computing in a Sulfonated Polyaniline Network”** | **Spoken digits, FSDD** | Physical **sulfonated-polyaniline organic electrochemical random network** | Up to about **70%**; all-speaker confusion matrix ≈**60%** | Physical electrochemical dynamics generate the reservoir state; software ridge regression performs classification. Particularly relevant to our hydrogel idea because memory capacity changed strongly with **humidity/ionic transport**. |
| **Mallinson et al., 2024 — “Experimental Demonstration of Reservoir Computing with Self-Assembled Percolating Networks of Nanoparticles”** | **Spoken digits, FSDD and TI-46** | Physical **Sn nanoparticle percolation network** near the percolation threshold | Entire FSDD: **3.5% WER = 96.5% accuracy** in the best parallel-reservoir configuration; TI-46 as low as **0.2% WER** with standard preprocessing | Physical nanoparticle network supplies the computation; **ridge-regression output weights are trained externally**. This is probably the closest existing nanoparticle-network analogue to what we are proposing. |

Zhu gives a very clean experimental MNIST result from a disordered Ag-containing network; Lilak directly demonstrates spoken-digit processing in an Ag atomic-switch network; and Mallinson demonstrates good spoken-digit performance in a self-assembled nanoparticle network, albeit with tin rather than silver.

In essentially all of these reservoir-computing examples, the material performs the nonlinear temporal transformation and provides memory, but a separate linear/ANN/readout layer learns the labels. Zhu trains RLS weights outside the material; Mallinson fits ridge-regression weights; Lilak uses offline regression; Usami uses ridge regression. Midya uses readout weights that are physically trained memristors, but the diffusive reservoir itself is still not trained.

# Silver nanoparticle device proposal

Here is a more specific proposal, although it is not the only plausible design.

**Summary**: This project will develop an adaptive physical learning system comprising a stationary, percolating network of silver-core/silver-sulfide-shell (Ag/Ag₂S) nanoparticles contacted by 16 independently addressable microelectrodes, combining materials engineering with hardware-in-the-loop machine learning to test whether task-beneficial transient changes in distributed conductive pathways can be selectively consolidated into persistent computational improvements.

**Device fabrication**: The initial device will use nominally 40–60 nm particles deposited on an insulating substrate with Pt-based electrodes defining an approximately 30 µm active region; particle loading, shell chemistry, contact morphology, and electrode geometry will be optimized for heterogeneous, junction-dominated conduction, reproducible nonlinear dynamics, and incremental plasticity rather than maximum conductivity. Silver-ion redistribution and conductive-bridge formation/dissolution are the candidate mechanisms for transient computational memory and retained adaptive state; voltage, pulse duration, repetition, polarity, and current compliance will be calibrated to distinguish computation, exploratory modification, consolidation, and recovery regimes. Electrical and electrochemical characterization will quantify relaxation spectra, stochastic switching, signal-to-noise ratio, drift, read disturbance, retention, reversibility, thermal loading, and endurance, with hardware-enforced current, charge, and energy limits.

**Machine learning**: On the ML side, the substrate will be treated as a partially observed, stochastic, nonstationary dynamical system whose latent state is accessible only through electrode excitation and terminal measurements, not as an array of individually addressable weights. Initial reservoir-computing baselines will use regularized linear readouts trained by ridge regression or recursive least squares; subsequent substrate adaptation will optimize a low-dimensional action space of electrode assignments, waveform-basis coefficients, pulse sequences, timing, and consolidation parameters. A state-aware black-box controller will infer compact latent-state representations from stimulation–response histories and select programming actions using contextual Bayesian optimization or Thompson-sampling bandits for short-horizon interventions, progressing to uncertainty-aware model-based reinforcement learning when action history, delayed rewards, and irreversible state changes require sequential credit assignment; SPSA and evolutionary strategies will provide derivative-free optimization baselines. Ensemble recurrent state-space surrogates will support system identification, predictive uncertainty estimation, and sample-efficient candidate selection within experimentally characterized operating limits, without assuming that a fitted terminal model uniquely identifies microscopic states. Rewards will combine task-loss reduction with penalties for programming energy, persistent drift, conductance saturation, and loss of useful response diversity; explicit stimulation budgets and interleaved reference probes will constrain exploration and detect nonstationarity.

The central learning hypothesis is that a weak programming intervention producing improved task performance can predict a corresponding retained improvement when its stimulation pattern is replayed under calibrated consolidation conditions, while unsuccessful interventions predominantly relax; exact reset, polarity-based reversibility, and autonomous junction-level policy gradients will not be assumed. Definitive internal-learning experiments will freeze input encoding, feature extraction, sampling, normalization, and readout weights, isolating material adaptation from digital decoder learning.

Benchmarks will include delayed parity, nonlinear temporal prediction, and adaptation to distribution shifts, evaluated on untouched test sequences across independent devices and sessions, with frozen-network, readout-only, shuffled-reward, energy-matched random-programming, and matched digital temporal-model controls.

**Simulation**: Supporting GPU-based simulations will couple stochastic electrical-network dynamics, ionic/filamentary state evolution, thermal feedback, parasitics, and acquisition models to compare learning algorithms and quantify sensitivity to device variability and model mismatch.

**Deliverables** will include reproducible fabrication protocols, an instrumented prototype, calibrated physical and terminal-level surrogate models, a hardware-in-the-loop training pipeline, and a controlled demonstration or falsification of retained task-directed adaptation, reporting generalization, sample efficiency, forgetting, retention, training and inference latency, system energy, and lifetime.

**Outcome**: The intended outcome is a reusable hybrid temporal learner in which the material supplies nonlinear computation, distributed memory, and persistent adaptation while an external controller supplies credit assignment and stability management, requiring coordinated expertise in nanoparticle/electrochemical materials, analog instrumentation, nonlinear system identification, reinforcement learning, Bayesian optimization, and noisy black-box control.

---

hypothesis: In an Ag/Ag₂S nanoparticle network, a weak electrode-induced modification that improves a task can predict a corresponding retained improvement after carefully increased stimulation of the same programming pattern. I would use chemically prepared Ag/Ag₂S core-shell nanoparticles deposited as a dry, interconnected film on an insulating substrate. The particles remain approximately stationary. Adaptation occurs primarily through changes at their contacts. Use fixed particles with mobile interfacial silver, and not freely moving particles in a colloid.

| Component       | Proposed first implementation                                                                                      |
| --------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| Active material | Ag/Ag₂S core-shell nanoparticles                                                                                   |
| Particle scale  | Approximately 40–60 nm nominal diameter; characterize the actual distribution and interfacial chemistry            |
| Substrate       | Oxidized silicon or equivalent insulating surface                                                                  |
| Contacts        | Sixteen Pt-based microelectrodes                                                                                   |
| Geometry        | Approximately 30 µm central active region, with neighboring contact gaps around 4–5 µm                             |
| Network         | Deposited, stationary particulate film; loading selected for reproducible, junction-dominated conduction           |
| Environment     | Controlled temperature and humidity; no liquid bath or intentionally mobile bulk particles                         |
| Electronics     | Programmable voltage waveforms, fast current compliance, simultaneous terminal acquisition, and a small controller |

hypothesis: Ag/Ag₂S networks can be organized into recurrent modules whose task-relevant state and retained plasticity remain controllable as the architecture grows, allowing token-level prediction errors to train increasingly expressive physical sequence representations.


